<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Basel</h1><p class="st-meta">1032 CE – 1803 CE (approx.) · capital Porrentruy</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A prince-bishopric on the upper Rhine whose bishops, driven out of Protestant Basel, ruled the Jura from Porrentruy until 1792.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-basel">Basel</a> had been a bishopric since the 7th century. In 999 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-iii-of-burgundy">King Rudolf III of Burgundy</a> gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> the abbey of Moutier-Grandval in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jura-mountains">Jura</a>, the basis of later territory. When <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Burgundy</a> fell to the Empire in 1032, Basel's bishops became imperial princes, later gaining further lands in the Jura and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> ruled the city of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/canton-of-basel">Basel</a> until its citizens won self-government, and Basel joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Swiss Confederacy</a> in 1501. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-basel">Council of Basel</a> met there from 1431, and the university was founded in 1460. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in 1529 the bishop moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porrentruy">Porrentruy</a>, ruling a mainly French-speaking territory.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>French revolutionary troops occupied the territory in 1792, creating the short-lived <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rauracian-republic">Rauracian Republic</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> annexed in 1793. The prince-bishopric was formally dissolved in 1803. In 1815 most of its lands were given to Bern, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jura-mountains">Jura</a> was created from part of them in 1979.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-basel.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Basel" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Roman_Catholic_Diocese_of_Basel.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1032 CE – 1803 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Porrentruy</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Jakob Christoph Blarer von Wartensee, Johann Konrad von Roggenbach</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Alemannic German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, <a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hochstift Basel, Bishopric of Basel</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1418&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-basel" aria-label="Prince-Bishopric of Basel on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4846 515 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Prince-Bishopric of Basel lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5166 771L5177 767M5166 771L5229 694M5166 771L5166 771M5166 771L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5177 767h0M5229 694h0M5166 771h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5166 771h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5166 771h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Porrentruy. <a href="/atlas?year=1418&amp;focus=prince-bishopric-of-basel">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

